Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{16}$ and then at $x=\frac{3 \pi}{8}$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{3 \pi}{8}\right)\\&=\frac{3}{4}\,\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{3}{4}\,\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{3 \sqrt{2}}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

